  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nA surface cold front is beginning to shift southward into far \nnorthern Illinois early this morning. This front will bring a \nnortheasterly wind shift as it settles into southern parts of \nthe area by late morning and afternoon. Cooler air filtering \ninto northern Illinois on breezy northeasterly winds will result\nin a cooler autumn day. Temperatures will top out only in the \nlower 60s across northeastern Illinois before falling back into \nthe 50s by early evening. Inland and further south, temperatures\nwill be warmer, with highs in the 70s expected south of I-80 \ninto central Illinois and Indiana. Skies look to remain mostly \ncloudy today, starting with a brief period of low cloud cover \n(less than 900 feet AGL) this morning, followed by persistent \nhigher-level clouds through the rest of the day.\n\nOn Tuesday, a series of mid-level impulses originating from \nnorthern Mexico are forecast to track northeastward into the \nlower Great Lakes, maintaining persistent mid- and high-level \nclouds across the area. The threat of rain remains very low due \nto dry low-level air; however, a few isolated sprinkles cannot \nbe ruled out during the afternoon and evening from high-based \nshowers. Potential impacts and confidence remain too low to \nwarrant a formal mention in the forecast.\n\nA large Canadian high pressure system will settle over New \nEngland by Wednesday. Flow around this high, combined with low \npressure troughing over the Plains and Upper Midwest, will \nreturn mild southerly winds to the region. Consequently, \ntemperatures will rebound back into the 70s area-wide by \nWednesday. While warmer weather will persist through the \nremainder of the week, there is a chance for isolated to \nscattered showers and possible thunderstorms late Wednesday \nnight into Thursday ahead of an approaching cold front.\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to indicate a pattern change late\nthis weekend into next week as a long-wave trough develops\nacross the western United States. This pattern could bring\nincreased rain chances to our region during and after the\nupcoming weekend.\n\nKJB",
